    & \shortstack{\texttt{Count}\\\texttt{down}}
    & \texttt{Python}
    & \texttt{MathIR}
    & \shortstack{\texttt{Pantry}\\\texttt{Plan}}
    & Avg \\
    \midrule
    \input{results/retention_matrix_panel_b_table_body.tex}
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{As outputs concentrate, rollouts under RLVR lose training signal.}
Dr.GRPO and MaxRL learn from a rollout group only when it contains a mix of correct and incorrect responses. Thus,
we audit every rollout group during training to track how often they have provided a non-zero learning signal. 
We find that Re:Max systematically increases the percentage of rollout groups with a learning signal (App.~\ref{app:degenerate-groups}); e.g., on \texttt{Python}, 18.5\% of groups have successes and failures under replay, compared to only 1.5\% without.

\subsection{Replay improves both accuracy and diversity}
\label{sec:results-retention}

\textbf{Replay improves accuracy and diversity across RLVR algorithms and model scales.}
We add our proposed verified-mode replay mechanism to Dr.GRPO and MaxRL and compare each against its matched no-replay control across five \mb{} domains and three model scales. Across these comparisons, we find that replay shifts the outcome toward both higher \texttt{pass@8} and greater reasoning-mode diversity (Fig.~\ref{fig:maxrl-factorial}; Tab.~\ref{tab:cross-scale-terminal-effects}).

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.65\textwidth}
  \centering
  \vspace{-0.9\baselineskip}
  \includegraphics[width=0.63\textwidth]{figures/e118_all_scale_factorial_progress.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Replay leads to higher accuracy and solution mode diversity.} Diamonds are initial LMs; open/filled markers are training without/with replay. (A) averages all five domains within seed; faint paths
  show seeds. (B) draws both arrows from the untrained model, one to the standard RLVR objective alone, one to it with replay, over the domains where every arm clears the support bar. App. Fig.~\ref{fig:maxrl-factorial-scale-extensions} provides results for each domain at every scale.}
  \label{fig:maxrl-factorial}
\end{wrapfigure}


\textbf{Mode diversity gains come from balancing retained modes.}
To isolate the role of mode balancing, we compare uniform replay with a frequency-weighted variant that rehearses modes in proportion to their discovery rate. Holding replay buffer construction, replay advantage scheduling, and loss update unchanged, we find that uniform weighting preserves greater verified diversity, most clearly in \texttt{Graph}, \texttt{Countdown}, and \texttt{PantryPlan}.
For example, the post-trained LM recovers roughly 0.6 additional modes per prompt in \texttt{Graph}, while also increasing \texttt{pass@8} by 6.6 percentage points.
Across domains, these gains come without an aggregate difference in correctness (App.~\ref{app:frequency-replay-progress}, Fig.~\ref{fig:replay-key-weighting}).

% BEGIN retained cited main figure
\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/replay_key_weighting.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Uniform weighting preserves more verified alternatives.}
Uniform minus frequency-weighted replay at Qwen2.5-0.5B, Level 1, with five matched seeds. Extra modes are $B_8=\texttt{distinct@8}-\texttt{pass@8}$. Bars show nominal 95\% paired-seed bootstrap intervals. The mean gain is $.318$ extra modes, with an inconclusive correctness effect. Extra modes remain coupled to correctness; they do not measure success-conditional \pmd{}.}
\label{fig:replay-key-weighting}
\end{figure}
% END retained cited main figure

\textbf{Alternative diversity mechanisms produce isolated rather than consistent gains.}
We compare Re:Max and Re:Dr.(ours) against UCPO \citep{lochab2026ucpo}, RLEP \citep{zhang2025rlep}, GAPO \citep{anschel2025groupaware}, SetPO \citep{li2026setpo}, and fixed Semantic-MaxEnt (Tab.~\ref{tab:direct-comparator-matrix}, App.~\ref{app:ucpo-progress}). 
Some of these methods produce substantial gains in individual domains, e.g., GAPO increases \pmd{} by $65.3$ percentage points in \texttt{PantryPlan}, while SetPO increases \texttt{pass@8} by $49.7$ percentage points in \texttt{Python}.
Yet these gains are domain-specific, while Re:Max improves both metrics across domains, averaging gains of $35.8$ percentage points in \texttt{pass@8} and $32.0$ percentage points in \pmd{} over Dr.GRPO.

