\subsection{Replay remains effective beyond the base training setting}
\label{sec:results-levels}

\textbf{Replay's gains persist on harder task constructions.}
We repeat the replay-versus-control comparison within each of the first three \mb{} levels, holding the level and fresh objective fixed. Replay improves average \texttt{pass@8} and \pmd{} under both Dr.GRPO and MaxRL at every level (Fig.~\ref{fig:level2-admission}).
At Level 2, for instance, replay increases \pmd{} on \texttt{Graph} by $0.314$ under Dr.GRPO and $0.212$ under MaxRL. Gains vary across domains, but remain positive in aggregate even at Level 3 (App.~\ref{app:level2-interim}).



\textbf{Replay leaves more usable alternative correct solutions when the task changes.}
We take a portfolio of eight generated answers, remove one option from the underlying task---e.g. an ingredient in \texttt{PantryPlan} or a color in \texttt{Graph}---and re-check the saved answers under the modified task.
Across all five domains, replay-trained policies are more likely to have an alternative answer that remains valid after removal. 
 When none of the saved answers remains valid, replay-trained policies also find a new valid answer in fewer additional attempts than ordinary or tuned-temperature sampling (Apps.~\ref{app:withdrawal-recovery},~\ref{app:pantry-adaptation}).






\textbf{Reference KL and replay preserve different sources of diversity.}
A potential explanation is that replay need not remember individual modes at all: perhaps anchoring the policy to its initially broader distribution is enough to prevent collapse.
To test this, we sweep the coefficient of a KL penalty to the initial policy and compare its
correctness--diversity curve with Re:Max/ Re:Dr.
The anchor is limited by the diversity already present in its reference, whereas replay is limited
by the modes its buffer discovers. On the four-domain aggregate, matching replay's diversity with
the anchor costs model correctness (Fig.~\ref{fig:reference-kl-plane}). We further show analytically that diversity depends on whether all modes were contained within the initial policy or discovered during training (App.~\ref{app:theory-kl}).

% \textbf{Takeaway.}
% Re:Max remains useful beyond the training setting. Replay gains persist on harder tasks, its retained modes provide valid fallbacks when constraints change, and it preserves discovered diversity rather than anchoring the policy to the diversity of the base.


\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.45\textwidth}
  \centering
  \vspace{-0.7\baselineskip}
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/reference_kl_plane.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Replay outperforms KL anchoring.}
  Means over five domains for Qwen2.5-0.5B. The anchor forms a curve because
  we sweep several $\beta$-KL coefficients. Increasing $\beta$ preserves more
  solution diversity, but at a lower \texttt{pass@8}.
  Green indicates where an arm achieves higher \texttt{pass@8} than the anchor
  at the same level of diversity
  (App.~\ref{app:theory-kl-measured}).}
  \label{fig:reference-kl-plane}
\end{wrapfigure}


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\textbf{Measuring solution diversity under alignment} RLHF and reasoning RL can reduce
output diversity, entropy, and high-budget coverage
\citep{kirk2024understanding,dang2025diversity,cui2025entropy,yue2025rlvrlimit}.
Measuring this collapse, however, requires defining when two correct answers differ meaningfully.
Semantic clustering infers it from language \citep{farquhar2024detecting}, which is general but depends on a judge.
\mb{} instead defines solution identity through the same executable check that is used to assign reward, such that two outputs differ exactly when they execute differently.
This gives us a narrower but cheaper and verifier-grounded measure of solution diversity, shared between training and evaluation. Preserving such alternatives matters because sampling-based selection relies on generating multiple viable proposals \citep{brown2024monkeys,snell2024testtime,cobbe2021training,lightman2023lets}.


% The literature establishes the impact of solution mode collapse. Our work tries to quantify the impact and establish both a benchmark for evaluation and a potential path forward to resolve solution mode collapse.

% \textbf{What is a different answer?} 
% Diversity can only be measured once sameness is defined.
% Semantic clustering infers it from language \citep{farquhar2024detecting}, which is general but depends on a judge.
% \mb{} instead defines solution identity through the same executable check that used to assign reward, such that two outputs differ exactly when they execute differently.

% narrower, cheaper, and shared by training and evaluation. 
% Alternatives matter because sampling-based selection consumes proposals
% \citep{brown2024monkeys,snell2024testtime,cobbe2021training,lightman2023lets}.

\textbf{Expanding diversity.} 
Outcome bonuses, set prediction, distribution matching, and uniform-correct objectives all reshape the learning signal\citep{song2025outcome,chen2025passktraining,li2025divergence,li2026setpo,li2026distribution,lochab2026ucpo}, while MaxRL combines objectives across sampling budgets \citep{tajwar2026maxrl}.
Yet these approaches still depend on what the current policy samples---precisely where our analysis finds that solution diversity disappears.
Memory can break this dependence on current samples, but what it preserves depends on how it replays experience.
Both standard replay~\citep{lin1992selfimproving,rolnick2019replay} and RLEP~\citep{zhang2025rlep} mitigate forgetting by revisiting successes in proportion to how their occurrences.
Instead, we homogeneously balance replay across solution modes, an idea related to explicitly preserving behavioral diversity in quality-diversity methods \citep{mouret2015illuminating,ecoffet2021return}.


% Balancing modes independently of discovery frequency separates our memory from those and connects it to behavioral archives in quality-diversity methods \citep{mouret2015illuminating,ecoffet2021return}.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:limitations}



We contribute \mb{}, a benchmark for studying solution mode diversity in RLVR, and Re:Max, a replay-based method that preserves discovered solution modes during post-training. 
Across objectives, model scales, and task difficulties, we find that replay can retain solution diversity while improving correctness. 
Our results further suggest that solution diversity may be itself a target for optimization. Future work can move beyond preserving discovered modes towards training models to produce desired distributions over ways to solve a problem.


% \mb{} makes accurate answering and diversity separately observable, and Re:Max keeps discovered successes in play after fresh sampling stops producing them. Given we show that model diversity can be retained via replay, follow up work should explore whether we can carry out post-training to produce a pre-determined / pre-desired output mode distribution or solution mode ordering.


\textbf{Limitations.} We acknowledge that replay retains only what sampling finds, thus why a more capable objective like MaxRL outperforms when combined with our replay mechanism. We also recognize that mode count says nothing about the ``worth'' of additional solutions. Our experiments are designed such that the equivalence is executable by construction, so future work is needed to evaluate the benefit (and feasibility) of replay in more open-ended solution spaces. 

