\section{Replay Algorithm}
\label{app:algorithm}

Verified replay combines a fresh task objective with uniform rehearsal over
stored solution modes. For each prompt $x$, $\mathcal B_x$ contains at most
$M$ verified policy exemplars, one per canonical key. A round-robin cursor
$\sigma$ selects one prompt bank per update. Table~\ref{tab:notation}
defines the notation; App.~\ref{app:replay-metric-alignment} relates the
replay objective to the diversity metric.


\subsection{Fresh task advantages}
\label{app:binary-maxrl}

For a group of $G$ fresh responses with verified binary rewards $R_i$ and
$R=\sum_i R_i$, Dr.GRPO uses $A_i=R_i-R/G$, while MaxRL uses
\[
 A_i^{\mathrm{MaxRL}}=
 \begin{cases}
 G R_i/R-1, & R>0,\\
 0, & R=0.
 \end{cases}
\]
The centered MaxRL estimator \citep{tajwar2026maxrl} gives failures advantage
$-1$ in mixed groups and weights each success more strongly when successes
are scarce. All-correct and all-failure groups have zero task advantage.
Lemma~\ref{lem:maxrl-mean} gives its harmonic pass@\(k\) mean objective.
These advantages apply only to fresh responses; bank exemplars enter the
separate verified-likelihood loss.

\subsection{Optimizer update}
\label{app:algorithm-update}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
  \caption{\textbf{Verified replay adds uniform rehearsal to fresh task learning.}
    One optimizer update with $o=\mathrm{Dr.GRPO}$ gives Re:Dr;
    $o=\mathrm{MaxRL}$ gives Re:Max.}
  \label{alg:verified-replay-update}
  \small
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \REQUIRE Policy $\pi_\theta$; prompt $x$ and verifier specification
      $s_x$; group size $G$; fresh objective
      $o\in\{\mathrm{MaxRL},\mathrm{Dr.GRPO}\}$; replay weight
      $\lambda_{\mathrm{replay}}$; states $(\mathcal B,\sigma)$
    \ENSURE Updated $(\theta',\mathcal B',\sigma')$
    \STATE Draw the on-policy group
      $\mathcal G=\{y_i\sim\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid x)\}_{i=1}^G$
    \FOR{$i=1,\ldots,G$}
      \STATE Execute $\widetilde c_i\gets V(y_i,s_x)$ and observe task
        reward $R_i$
      \STATE Set $c_i\gets\widetilde c_i$ iff
        $R_i>0\wedge\widetilde c_i\ne\bot$; otherwise set $c_i\gets\bot$
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE Compute fresh task advantages $A_i^{o}$ from $\{R_j\}_{j=1}^G$;
      for $o=\mathrm{MaxRL}$ use the formula in App.~\ref{app:binary-maxrl}
    \STATE After the full group is validated, deterministically retain one
      policy exemplar for each new $c_i\ne\bot$ in $\mathcal B_x$, up to
      capacity $M$
    \STATE $(z,\sigma')\gets\textsc{NextBank}
      (\{\mathcal B_u\}_u,\sigma)$ by a fixed round-robin cursor over
      prompts; the replayed prompt $z$ need not be the prompt $x$ that
      supplied the fresh group
    \IF{a replay bank $\mathcal B_z$ exists}
      \STATE For each exemplar $e_c\in\mathcal B_z$, compute
        $\ell_c=|e_c|^{-1}\log\pi_\theta(e_c\mid z)$
      \STATE $L_{\mathrm{replay}}\gets-|\mathcal B_z|^{-1}
        \sum_{c\in\mathcal B_z}\ell_c$
    \ELSE
      \STATE $L_{\mathrm{replay}}\gets0$
    \ENDIF
    \STATE $w_{\mathrm{rep}}\gets(G-1)/G^2$ for one replay
      pseudo-group per $G$ on-policy rows
    \STATE $\theta'\gets\textsc{Update}
      (\theta,L_{\mathrm{PPO}}(A^{o})
      +w_{\mathrm{rep}}\lambda_{\mathrm{replay}}L_{\mathrm{replay}})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Admission, capacity, and deterministic scheduling}
\label{app:algorithm-bank}

Each bank corresponds to one exact prompt-token sequence. A response is
eligible when it has positive task reward and a valid canonical key;
responses truncated at the generation cap are subject to the same rule.
All responses in a group are validated before any new key is added. For each
new key, the lexicographically smallest normalized response-token tuple is
retained; new keys are admitted in sorted-key order. Admission is therefore
invariant to rollout-row order. A bank stores one exemplar per key, up to
$M=16$, with no eviction or replacement. Once capacity is reached, later
keys receive no replay support.

At each update, a deterministic round-robin schedule selects one nonempty
bank; singleton banks are eligible. Selection is
independent of reward, evaluation, desired support, and entropy. The selected
bank is scored by teacher forcing on its original prompts and exemplars.
Each key contributes its exemplar's mean response-token log probability,
excluding prompt and padding tokens. These scores
are averaged uniformly across the selected bank and multiplied by
$\lambda_{\mathrm{replay}}(G-1)/G^2$, as in
Algorithm~\ref{alg:verified-replay-update}. The factor $(G-1)/G^2$ equals the
weight of a lone success in a centered Dr.GRPO group: its advantage is
$(G-1)/G$, and averaging over the $G$ responses supplies the additional
$1/G$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:grpo-mean}). Neither exhaustive support nor evaluation
outputs enter the replay loss.

\subsection{Zero-derivative replay control}
\label{app:algorithm-control}

Replay and its control share validation, bank admission, scheduling, and
replay scoring. The control sets the replay score-gradient vector to exact
zeros while retaining the replay forward and backward passes. Fresh-task
and replay gradients accumulate before the same optimizer step; the
replay derivative is the only difference in the learning objective.
Subsequent policies can produce different samples, bank contents, selected
banks, and exemplar lengths. Optimizer-update and fresh-rollout budgets are
matched, but realized FLOPs and wall-clock time are not. The control also
does not allocate replay computation to additional task-gradient updates,
fresh rollouts, or longer training. It isolates the replay learning signal
without establishing superiority at equal total compute
(App.~\ref{app:compute-matching}).

\subsection{Verifier requirements and applicability}
\label{app:algorithm-identity}

Replay requires a deterministic equivalence relation on verified outputs.
The canonical key must be computable during training, inexpensive enough to
evaluate for every response, and consistent with the task's notion of a
solution. In \mb{}, the executable verifier supplies both correctness and
canonical identity, so admission requires no learned judge or embedding
threshold.

Final-answer grading on tasks such as MATH, GSM8K, and AIME does not identify
distinct derivations. For a prompt with one correct answer, an answer-based
key maps every successful response to the same mode. Its bank then holds
one exemplar, and replay reinforces that exemplar without balancing
alternative derivations. Distinguishing derivations requires a richer
identity. \texttt{MathIR} uses executed state trajectories and includes certified
four-state routes. None appears among \MIRdraws{} verified draws. Among the
\MIRsolvedprompts{} solved Level-1 prompts, these draws cover
\MIRcovershort{}\% of the certified two-state modes and \MIRcovermid{}\%
of the three-state modes. These modes rarely coexist within a prompt.
\texttt{MathIR}'s terminal \pmd{} averages \MIRpmdafter{}, equivalent to
\MIRneffafter{} effective verified modes per prompt. These results limit the
evidence for replay on derivation-based identities.

Across the \MIRshiftcells{} five-domain comparisons, including \texttt{MathIR}
reduces the mean gain by \MIRshiftmin{}--\MIRshiftmax{} \pmd{} relative to
the corresponding four-domain mean. At Qwen2.5-0.5B, the Re:Dr gain is \MIRfive{} over all five
domains and \MIRfour{} over the remaining four domains.
App.~\ref{app:hosted-mode-diversity} reports results for the same four-domain subset.

For open-ended derivations without an executable identity, a learned judge
could supply a novelty criterion. Such a criterion requires separate
validation: judge decisions may be inconsistent, may disagree with task
correctness, and may reward distinctions that replay can exploit without
improving solution diversity. A fixed model, prompt, and decoding rule alone
do not establish a valid equivalence relation. Agreement with executable
keys can be evaluated where both are available, while diversity measured by
those keys remains independent of the judge's training signal. Judge-based
replay is not evaluated here.

\subsection{Exemplar quality and length}
\label{app:algorithm-quality}

Canonical verification establishes output validity, not the quality of every
intermediate reasoning step. A verified exemplar may be verbose or contain
an unsound explanation. Uniform replay assigns weight by solution key and
does not rank exemplars by reasoning quality.

Each exemplar contributes its mean response-token log probability,
$\ell_c=|e_c|^{-1}\log\pi_\theta(e_c\mid z)$, and each key occupies one bank
entry. Thus a longer response receives neither additional entries nor loss
weight proportional to its token count. In the categorical surrogate with
one fixed-length exemplar per key, the corresponding key weights are
proportional to $L_b^{-1}$; the full-coverage limit favors shorter exemplars
and is uniform only at equal lengths (App.~\ref{app:theory-replay}). This
surrogate does not imply uniform key probabilities in a language model.
Mean-token normalization also does not guarantee concise or high-quality
explanations.

Additional quality criteria, such as a token budget or trace-quality score,
would change admission, exemplar selection, or eviction and hence the replay
target. They are not used in these comparisons. A brevity filter can remove
valid alternatives: \texttt{MathIR} includes longer state trajectories, and
\texttt{Python}'s rarer return vectors require longer lambdas than its modal
vector. Any such filter would need evaluation of both solution quality and
diversity among the retained modes.

\section{Fixed Semantic MaxEnt Comparator}
\label{app:semantic-maxent}

Fixed Semantic MaxEnt augments the fresh-rollout advantage using the
estimated frequency of each verified solution mode. It retains counts of
successful canonical keys but supplies no likelihood gradient on stored
exemplars. For row $i$ in a group of $G$ responses to prompt $x$, let
$n_x(a)$ count previous successful responses with key $a$, and let
$m_{-i}(a)$ count successful peers with that key. Define
$N_x=\sum_a n_x(a)$, $S_{-i}=\sum_a m_{-i}(a)\le G-1$, and let
$\mathcal K_i$ be the union of historical and peer-observed keys. Unit
pseudocounts and one reserved unseen bucket give, for $a\in\mathcal K_i$,
\begin{equation}
  \hat p_i(a)=\frac{n_x(a)+m_{-i}(a)+1}{N_x+S_{-i}+|\mathcal K_i|+1},\qquad
  \hat p_i(\textsc{unseen})=\frac{1}{N_x+S_{-i}+|\mathcal K_i|+1}.
  \label{eq:open-set-predictor}
\end{equation}
A sampled key outside $\mathcal K_i$ receives the unseen-bucket probability.
Unsuccessful and invalid peers contribute no count.

\paragraph{Prompt-specific success counts.}
Counts are maintained separately for each exact prompt-token sequence and
accumulate throughout training without decay or reset. Only responses with
positive reward and a valid key contribute. Each response is scored using
preceding groups and its successful leave-one-out peers; the current group
is added afterward. A response therefore never contributes to its own
predictor.
The added detached advantage on an eligible successful row is
\begin{equation}
  A^{\rm sem}_{i,t}=\eta\,
  \frac{\min\{-\log\hat p_i(a_i),\kappa\}
    -\E_{\hat p_i}[\min\{-\log\hat p_i(a),\kappa\}]}{\kappa},
  \qquad \eta=.10,\ \kappa=5.
  \label{eq:legacy-semantic-advantage}
\end{equation}
The score does not depend on the token index, so row $i$ carries the same
$A^{\rm sem}_{i,t}$ at every $t$. Invalid and unsuccessful rows receive zero,
and the centered score is bounded by $\eta$ in magnitude. It can favor an
underrepresented verified outcome when that outcome appears in a fresh
rollout, but supplies no gradient for an absent stored exemplar. If neither
history nor successful peers provide a key, the unseen bucket has
probability one and the bonus is zero. App.~\ref{app:ucpo-progress} reports
the empirical comparison.

For the true conditional distribution $q_\theta=p_\theta(\cdot\mid\mathcal C)$,
\begin{equation}
 \nabla_\theta H(q_\theta)
 =\E_{a\sim q_\theta}[(-\log q_\theta(a))\nabla_\theta\log q_\theta(a)].
 \label{eq:entropy-score-function}
\end{equation}
The empirical predictor does not yield an unbiased estimator of this
entropy gradient. Hold a detached predictor score vector
$s$ and baseline $b$ fixed independently of the scored action, for example
conditional on its leave-one-out peers. Averaging successful rows under the
unconditional policy then gives
\[
 \E_{Z\sim p}[\1\{Z\in\mathcal C\}(s_Z-b)\nabla_\theta\log p_Z]
 =P\E_{c\sim q}[s_c\nabla_\theta\log q_c]
   +(\E_q[s]-b)\nabla_\theta P.
\]
The predictor baseline need not equal the current conditional mean, so
this bonus can change correctness as well as diversity. Even with the exact
unclipped score $s_c=-\log q_c$ and baseline $b=H(q)$, averaging over
unconditional rollout rows gives $P\nabla_\theta H(q)$.
For a single correct key, conditional entropy is zero, yet the unseen
bucket can give that key negative semantic advantage once it has been
observed. The comparator
thus carries neither an exact entropy-gradient interpretation nor an
individual-mode preservation guarantee.

