Tables~\ref{tab:core-terminal-endpoints} and~\ref{tab:cross-scale-terminal-effects}
show absolute arm means and paired replay effects, respectively. Dr.GRPO
replay increases mean \texttt{pass@8} and \distk{} in all 14 five-seed
comparisons; \distk{} increases in all 74 terminal seed pairs.
Because \distk{} depends on correctness as well as diversity, \pmd{} provides
a separate conditional measure.

At Qwen2.5-0.5B, \texttt{Countdown} \texttt{pass@8} rises from $.480$ to $.672$,
while \pmd{} rises by $+.480$ (95\% interval $[+.447,+.513]$). \texttt{Python}
correctness rises from $.172$ to $.528$, with a diversity effect of $+.082$
($[-.146,+.310]$); \texttt{MathIR} rises from $.512$ to $.796$, with a diversity effect
of $+.017$ ($[+.003,+.031]$). These are nominal, unadjusted Student-$t$
intervals over five paired training seeds. \texttt{Countdown}'s terminal \pmd{}
values are $.007$ for Dr.GRPO and $.487$ for Re:Dr. Its keys distinguish
computation routes, although these held-out endpoints do not identify which
previously discovered routes survived training.

Fig.~\ref{fig:maxrl-factorial-scale-extensions} gives the absolute results
by domain. Pairwise effects use seeds shared by the two arms; factorial
interactions use seeds shared by all four methods. Diversity additionally
requires enough verified responses to define the conditional estimator.

