At Qwen2.5-0.5B, Re:Max raises \pmd{} by $+.439$, $+.489$, and $+.317$ on
\texttt{Graph}, \texttt{Countdown}, and \texttt{PantryPlan}, respectively; each five-seed interval
excludes zero. Falcon \texttt{Graph} has a negative mean diversity effect of $-.009$
($[-.034,+.016]$) and an inconclusive correctness effect of $+.014$
($[-.016,+.044]$). Positive cross-domain averages therefore do not imply a
gain in every domain. At Qwen2.5-3B, the five-seed diversity effects are
$+.076$, $+.077$, $+.058$, $+.006$, and $+.224$ for \texttt{Graph}, \texttt{Countdown}, \texttt{Python},
\texttt{MathIR}, and \texttt{PantryPlan}. Every interval excludes zero, although the \texttt{MathIR}
gain is small. Correctness intervals exclude zero for \texttt{Countdown}, \texttt{MathIR},
and \texttt{PantryPlan}; \texttt{Graph} and \texttt{Python} are inconclusive. Re:Max has higher mean
\pmd{} than MaxRL in \PMDaboveReMax{} of \PMDblocksReMax{} measurable
comparisons, and Re:Dr exceeds Dr.GRPO in \PMDaboveReDr{} of
\PMDblocksReDr{}. These counts describe the direction of the means, not
statistical significance.

\paragraph{Cross-domain factorial summaries.}
\label{app:factorial-numerical-detail}
Averaging five domains equally within each paired seed, Re:Max minus MaxRL
improves \texttt{pass@8} by $+.307$ at Qwen2.5-0.5B (95\% interval
$[+.208,+.406]$), $+.133$ at Falcon3-1B ($[+.043,+.223]$), and $+.154$ at
Qwen2.5-3B ($[+.086,+.222]$), each using five seeds. Re:Dr minus Dr.GRPO
improves correctness by $+.337$, $+.442$, and $+.279$, respectively. The
Falcon Dr.GRPO average uses four common seeds and has no interval; the other
two use five.

At Qwen2.5-3B, MaxRL/Re:Max arm means are $.547/.701$ for correctness and
$.744/1.107$ for \distk{}. The paired raw-count effect is $+.363$
($[+.285,+.441]$). Cross-domain \pmd{} averages in
Table~\ref{tab:cross-scale-terminal-effects} use a fixed set of domains
supported by every seed.

