\subsection{Supporting comparators}
\label{app:ucpo-progress}

GRPO changes group-reward normalization; UCPO redistributes fresh on-policy
advantage; sparse RLEP-Dr reuses verified successes without uniform weighting
by canonical key. GRPO has 75 terminal runs across the three model scales.
UCPO and RLEP-Dr use the two smaller scales, with 50 and 47 terminal runs,
respectively; RLEP has two seeds for Falcon \texttt{Python} and five elsewhere.
Fixed Semantic MaxEnt provides a fresh-rollout frequency bonus
(App.~\ref{app:semantic-maxent}). The four-arm semantic factorial covers ten model--domain cells at the
two smaller scales, with five common seeds except Falcon \texttt{Countdown} ($n=4$). The 3B comparison uses one seed and tests adding the bonus to replay
(App.~\ref{app:semantic-current-factorial}).

The GRPO, UCPO, and RLEP diversity contrasts in this subsection average
per-prompt differences over prompts where both arms have at least two
correct responses, requiring at least 30 such common prompts per seed.
This differs from Table~\ref{tab:direct-comparator-matrix}, which uses
separate 20-prompt thresholds per arm. On Qwen2.5-0.5B \texttt{Graph}, one UCPO seed
and four RLEP seeds meet the common-prompt criterion; no \texttt{Python} comparison
at any scale meets it.

At Qwen2.5-0.5B, UCPO raises \texttt{Countdown} \texttt{pass@8} by $+.133$
($[+.005,+.262]$), with an inconclusive \pmd{} effect of $+.002$
($[-.018,+.023]$). Its \texttt{MathIR} effects are also inconclusive. RLEP-Dr raises
\texttt{MathIR} correctness by $+.126$ ($[+.030,+.222]$), with zero measured diversity
change; its \texttt{Countdown} and Falcon \texttt{PantryPlan} effects are inconclusive.
Across GRPO, UCPO, and RLEP, seven of 34 complete five-seed correctness
intervals lie above zero. None of 19 complete diversity intervals does;
one lies below zero, for Falcon \texttt{Countdown} GRPO: $-.105$ ($[-.147,-.063]$).
At Qwen2.5-3B, all five GRPO correctness intervals and the three available
diversity intervals include zero. Intervals are nominal paired 95\%
Student-$t$ intervals over training seeds.

Uniform versus frequency-weighted replay uses nine five-seed comparisons:
all five Qwen2.5-0.5B domains and \texttt{Graph}/\texttt{PantryPlan} at both larger scales.
Changing only the within-bank weights adds $+.318$ extra verified modes per
prompt in the Qwen2.5-0.5B aggregate ($[+.272,+.358]$, paired-seed bootstrap),
with an inconclusive correctness effect of $+.010$ ($[-.119,+.139]$).
This supports an additional benefit from balancing beyond frequency-weighted
reuse; extra-mode counts remain coupled to correctness
(App.~\ref{app:frequency-replay-progress}).

RLEP's offline pool uses temperature $.7$ and top-$p=.95$, whereas evaluation
uses temperature 1 and top-$p=1$. Offline collection also differs from the
online bank construction used by verified replay.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/direct_baseline_learning_curves_pass8.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Re:Dr has the highest terminal mean accuracy in every
  displayed comparison.} \texttt{pass@8} during Level-1 training for Dr.GRPO,
  Re:Dr, UCPO, and sparse RLEP-Dr. Rows show Qwen2.5-0.5B and Falcon3-1B;
  columns show the five domains. Lines show seed means and bands their ranges;
  gaps indicate unavailable estimates. Panel labels give sample sizes: five
  except Falcon \texttt{Countdown} Dr.GRPO/Re:Dr ($n=4$) and Falcon
  \texttt{Python} RLEP-Dr ($n=2$). Dr.GRPO/Re:Dr use paired seeds.}
  \label{fig:direct-baseline-accuracy-curves}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Diversity-preserving comparators}
\label{app:diversity-comparators}

GAPO~\citep{anschel2025groupaware} and SetPO~\citep{li2026setpo} target
output diversity within the fresh rollout group. Both are evaluated on
Level-1 Qwen2.5-0.5B across five domains and five seeds. GAPO assigns
verifier-positive rollouts the reward $[1-(f_i-1/L)+1]/2$, with zero for
failures, where $f_i$ is the key's frequency among the group's verified
rollouts and $L$ is the prompt's certified number of valid modes.
SetPO adds each rollout's leave-one-out marginal contribution to kernelized
set diversity to its advantage, with coefficient $\lambda=1$.
It uses frozen \texttt{all-MiniLM-L6-v2} sentence embeddings with mean pooling
and cosine similarity clamped to $[0,1]$. The embedding forward pass adds
computation.

GAPO increases \pmd{} on \texttt{Countdown} ($+.340$, $[+.112,+.567]$) and \texttt{PantryPlan}
($+.653$, $[+.610,+.696]$), while its \texttt{PythonFactors} effect is near zero
with an interval spanning zero. \texttt{PythonFactors} has more valid modes than the
sixteen-rollout group can represent on 95\% of training prompts, with up to 3,600
modes. This limits within-group coverage but does not prevent a policy from
assigning mass across the full support. \texttt{MathIR} effects are small across
methods; the largest is Re:Dr's $+.017$ ($[+.003,+.031]$).
SetPO increases \pmd{} on \texttt{PantryPlan} ($+.266$, $[+.177,+.356]$); its other
four domain intervals include zero. Five-domain mean effects are $+.225$
for GAPO, $+.066$ for SetPO, and $+.320$ for Re:Max, although GAPO's
\texttt{PantryPlan} effect is larger than Re:Max's. GAPO reshapes rewards using fresh
group frequencies, whereas replay balances rehearsal across a bank of
verified modes accumulated over training.

GAPO and SetPO share a separate Dr.GRPO control under their hardware and
disjoint-draw evaluation configuration. Its terminal \pmd{} differs from
the primary control by a mean of $+.003$ over twenty paired cells, with a
range of $-.014$ to $+.034$. These observed differences are small relative
to the five-domain effects above, but do not establish equivalence between
the controls.

\subsection{Uniform versus fresh-frequency replay}
\label{app:frequency-replay-progress}

The weighting ablation uses the same bank construction and update rule,
changing only the within-bank target: uniform weights over retained keys
versus weights proportional to verifier-positive fresh-rollout counts.
Policy-dependent bank contents can subsequently differ. Positive contrasts
below favor uniform weighting. The two primary outcomes are
$B_8=\texttt{distinct@8}-\texttt{pass@8}$, extra verified modes per prompt,
and $P_8=\texttt{pass@8}$. The five-domain Qwen2.5-0.5B aggregate averages
domain effects within each matched seed, giving equal domain weight.

Complete-block intervals enumerate all $5^5=3{,}125$ ordered bootstrap
resamples of the five paired training seeds and use linearly interpolated
2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. Each resampled seed carries its complete domain
vector for the five-domain aggregate. These nominal intervals quantify seed
variability within the fixed benchmark; prompts and domains are not resampled,
and intervals are not adjusted across comparisons.

The Qwen2.5-0.5B five-domain effect is $+.318$ extra modes per
prompt ($[+.272,+.358]$), with positive within-seed domain averages in all
five seeds. \texttt{Graph}, \texttt{Countdown}, and \texttt{PantryPlan} drive this result; \texttt{Python} and
\texttt{MathIR} remain inconclusive. The $P_8$ effect is $+.010$
($[-.119,+.139]$), establishing neither a gain nor equivalence. \texttt{Python}'s
$P_8$ effects range from $-.781$ to $+.828$ across seeds despite
small extra-mode effects in four seeds.

Falcon \texttt{Graph} also favors uniform weighting on extra modes ($+.233$,
$[+.182,+.320]$). Its correctness measures differ: $\Delta P_8=+.045$
($[-.0004,+.0910]$) is inconclusive, while mean per-response correctness
falls by $-.0152$ ($[-.0214,-.0090]$), with an interval entirely below zero.
Falcon \texttt{Pantry} is inconclusive on both outcomes:
$\Delta B_8=-.146$ ($[-.564,+.414]$) and $\Delta P_8=-.021$
($[-.063,+.026]$). Qwen2.5-3B \texttt{Graph} has five pairs and an extra-mode effect of
$+.079$ ($[+.011,+.135]$); its $P_8$ interval includes zero.
Qwen2.5-3B \texttt{Pantry} also has five pairs, with $\Delta B_8=+.207$
($[+.038,+.377]$) and inconclusive $\Delta P_8$ ($+.011$, $[-.020,+.043]$).

Rehearsing self-generated verified successes is related to rejection-sampling
fine-tuning~\citep{dong2023raft}; here the likelihood term is length-normalized
and combined with the on-policy objective. This ablation isolates the effect
of uniform versus fresh-frequency weighting within that replay procedure.
It supports an additional sampled-diversity benefit in the stated domains.
The inconclusive $P_8$ effects do not establish that weighting leaves accuracy
unchanged or that replay's accuracy and diversity effects have separate causes.

