\subsection{Diversity before and after RLVR-only training}
\label{app:pre-rl-regression}

Unmodified Dr.GRPO and GRPO are evaluated at passes 0 and 8
on all three models and five domains: 150 runs, each evaluated with four
$K=8$ groups per checkpoint. The five-domain macro-average of extra verified
modes falls by $98.2\%$--$99.4\%$ at Qwen2.5-0.5B and
$41.5\%$--$71.6\%$ at larger scales. The \texttt{pass@8} mean falls in
10 of the 30 domain--method--scale comparisons, while macro
\texttt{distinct@8} ends above its initial value at Qwen2.5-3B.
The loss of multiple sampled correct outcomes can therefore coexist with
increased probability of sampling at least one correct outcome. These finite
observations do not establish zero probability for unobserved keys.

Macro \pmd{} uses domains measurable at both endpoints: \texttt{Graph}, \texttt{MathIR}, and
\texttt{PantryPlan} at 0.5B; \texttt{Graph}, \texttt{Countdown}, and \texttt{PantryPlan} at 1B; and \texttt{Graph} and
\texttt{PantryPlan} at 3B. It declines by $98.5\%$ and $99.7\%$ at Qwen2.5-0.5B under
GRPO and Dr.GRPO, respectively, and by $50.8\%$--$65.1\%$ at the larger
scales. Terminal macro \pmd{} rounds to $.001$ for Dr.GRPO at Qwen2.5-0.5B.
Thus the measured decline also appears in diversity conditional on success.
Eligibility depends on the number of correct samples, so these descriptive
macro-averages do not hold the prompt population or correctness fixed;
App.~\ref{app:conditional-concentration} reports contrasts on jointly eligible
prompts. Falcon3-1B \texttt{Countdown} is an exception to the Dr.GRPO decline:
its \pmd{} rises from $.033$.

Each method uses its own initial evaluation, so finite-sample initial
estimates can differ despite shared model weights.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/baseline_collapse_precheck.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{RLVR-only training reduces average extra verified modes
  at every tested scale.} Bars decompose \texttt{distinct@8} into
  \texttt{pass@8} and extra verified modes before training and after eight
  passes. Panels show Dr.GRPO and GRPO across three models and five domains:
  150 runs, with five seeds per cell and four eight-response groups per
  checkpoint. Whiskers show the seed range; each method uses its own
  initial evaluation. Badges give the percentage change in extra modes only
  where the initial mean exceeds $.05$; the other three domains start at
  $.011$ or less.}
  \label{fig:baseline-precheck}
\end{figure}




\texttt{Graph} and \texttt{PantryPlan} contain substantial initial diversity at every scale;
the other three domains begin with at most $.011$ extra modes per prompt.
A retained-diversity percentage is therefore reported only when the initial
extra-mode count exceeds $.05$. Raw $\texttt{distinct@8}$ ends below its
initial value in six of the 15 Dr.GRPO domains. Re:Dr does so in
two available domain summaries, both \texttt{PantryPlan}; Falcon \texttt{Countdown} uses
four seeds. Conditional diversity comparisons depend on which prompts produce
enough correct responses. App.~\ref{app:conditional-concentration} gives
paired contrasts on jointly eligible prompts, with their seed counts and
uncertainty. These held-out measurements do not track the survival of
individual stored modes.

Uniform-action references are meaningful for the enumerable \texttt{Graph} and
\texttt{Pantry} representations, and App.~\ref{app:metric-sampling} derives them. No uniform-action
reference is specified for the other domains. \texttt{Pantry}'s bit-uniform reference exceeds every final model's sampled
\texttt{distinct@8}; the trained policies do not approach that reference.

