\subsection{Fresh-gradient degeneracy and observed output collapse}
\label{app:degenerate-groups}

A Dr.GRPO group with identical rewards has exactly zero fresh task gradient.
Only mixed groups containing both correct and incorrect responses can
supply fresh reward information; optimizer momentum can still move a
policy when that task gradient is zero. The fractions of mixed groups
over all 3,072 Qwen2.5-0.5B updates, averaged over five seeds, are:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Dr.GRPO} & \textbf{Re:Dr} \\
\midrule
\texttt{Graph} coloring & .144 & .954 \\
\texttt{Countdown} & .140 & .456 \\
\texttt{Python} factors & .015 & .185 \\
\texttt{MathIR} & .364 & .453 \\
\texttt{PantryPlan} & .123 & .789 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Thus 98.5\% of \texttt{Python} control updates carry zero fresh task gradient.
Replay supplies a verified-likelihood gradient directly and can also
change the policy so that later fresh groups become mixed.

All five Qwen2.5-0.5B \texttt{Python} control seeds exhibit observed output collapse.
At pass 0, each eight-response group contains an average of 7.99 unique raw
completions per prompt. By steps 288--480, all 32 recorded temperature-1
responses per prompt are byte-identical within each seed and match its greedy
completion, on all 128 prompts. This persists at every subsequent evaluation
through step 3,072. The 32 responses span four groups with overlapping sampling
streams (App.~\ref{app:conditional-concentration}); they are not 32 independent
draws. Within eight-response groups, the terminal mean is 1.00 unique
completion per prompt for the control and 1.97 for replay. The repeated
completion can be incorrect. These finite observations do not rule out
low-probability alternatives.

This sparse binary-reward setting allows fresh task gradients to vanish.
Dynamic mixed-group sampling, larger groups, reference KL, token-entropy
regularization, and wider decoding could alter that behavior; their effects
are not identified by this factorial.

\subsection{Control behavior at fixed learning rates}
\label{app:control-learning-audit}

The controls use a constant learning rate of $2\times10^{-7}$ at
Qwen2.5-0.5B and Falcon3-1B and a peak rate of $10^{-7}$ with cosine decay
at Qwen2.5-3B (Table~\ref{tab:run-contract}). No learning-rate sweep is
available. Table~\ref{tab:control-learning-audit} describes how accuracy and
fresh reward information change at these operating points. The comparisons
establish effects under the tested schedules, without identifying the best
schedule for either arm.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{\textbf{Per-response accuracy increases in every control cell,
  while \texttt{pass@8} can fall.} Accuracy uses each run's initial and last
  available evaluations. Control and replay columns are unpaired means of
  five runs per cell. \texttt{mean@8} is per-response
  correctness. Mixed gives the mean fraction of recorded rollout groups
  containing both correct and incorrect responses. An unmixed group has
  zero fresh task advantage, although optimizer momentum can still change
  the policy. Training schedules match update counts and fresh-rollout
  budgets; total compute is discussed in App.~\ref{app:compute-accounting}.}
  \label{tab:control-learning-audit}
  \small
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    & & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{control \texttt{pass@8}}}
      & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{control \texttt{mean@8}}}
      & \textbf{mixed} & \textbf{Re:Dr} \\
    \cmidrule(lr){3-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}
    \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Domain} & initial & terminal & $\Delta$
      & initial & terminal & groups & \texttt{pass@8} \\
    \midrule
    \input{results/control_learning_table_body.tex}
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Accuracy changes during training.}
Of \MDctlCells{} control cells, \MDctlGains{} finish above their initial
\texttt{pass@8} and \MDctlLosses{} below it, with a mean change of
\MDctlMeanDelta{}. The largest increase is $+\MDctlBestGain{}$
(\MDctlBestGainCell{}); the largest decrease is $-\MDctlWorstLoss{}$
(\MDctlWorstLossCell{}). Per-response correctness rises in all
\MDctlMeanUp{} cells, including \MDctlYue{} where \texttt{pass@8} does not
increase. In \MDctlPatternCell{}, per-response correctness rises from
\MDctlPatternMeanFrom{} to \MDctlPatternMeanTo{}, while \texttt{pass@8}
falls from \MDctlPatternPassFrom{} to \MDctlPatternPassTo{}.
This combination is consistent with prior observations of increased
single-response accuracy alongside reduced sampling coverage
\citep{yue2025rlvrlimit}.

The gap between \texttt{pass@8} and \texttt{mean@8} is at least $.02$ in
\MDctlOpened{} initial cells and below $.02$ in \MDctlClosed{} terminal
cells. This gap measures variation in correctness within response groups;
it does not by itself identify repeated strings or conditional diversity
among correct keys. The raw-completion observations in
App.~\ref{app:degenerate-groups} establish repetition directly for the
Qwen2.5-0.5B \texttt{Python} controls.

\paragraph{Sparse fresh reward information.}
Initial \texttt{pass@8} is zero on \MDctlZeroBaseDomain{} at all three
scales. In \MDctlLeanestCell{}, only a fraction \MDctlLeanestMixed{} of
updates contain mixed rewards, leaving zero fresh task advantage in
\MDctlLeanestZero{}\% of updates. A learning-rate change cannot make an
identically zero current task gradient nonzero, but it can change the effect
of informative updates, optimizer momentum, and subsequent sampling.
The Falcon3-1B \texttt{Python} control's terminal \texttt{pass@8} rounds to $.000$, while Re:Dr
ends at $.979$. This is an accuracy contrast in a sparse-success setting;
conditional mode diversity requires at least two correct responses per
prompt and is undefined when that sampling condition is unmet.

\paragraph{Scope of the comparison.}
Replay and control share the learning rate, schedule, initialization,
prompt stream, optimizer, update count, and fresh-rollout budget.
Their differences therefore measure the effect of replay at the tested
operating point. These controls do learn, as measured by their increased
per-response correctness, but that does not establish adequate tuning or
exclude a stronger baseline. A different learning rate could change accuracy,
diversity, and the relative benefit of replay. Neither the sign nor the
magnitude of those changes is determined without a learning-rate comparison.

\subsection{Fixed-bank exemplar score trajectories}
\label{app:fixed-bank-survival}

The fixed-bank study follows Qwen2.5-0.5B Re:Dr on \texttt{Graph} with five
training seeds. Bank membership and fresh counts are fixed before step 384's
fresh rollout group. No further modes are added, and uniform replay
continues through step 3,072. Every exemplar in the fixed bank is tracked
by prompt and canonical mode. At each replay visit, its mean token log
probability $s$ and sequence log probability $\ell$ are evaluated.
Changes compare each exemplar's first and final visits after the bank is
fixed; visit steps can differ across exemplars.

The 3,406 exemplars span 1,575 seed--prompt pairs, with 8--9 replay visits
per exemplar.
The pooled median change is $+.038$ nat/token (95\% interval
$[+.025,+.048]$), while the 10th percentile is $-.256$ and $2.61\%$ of
exemplars lose more than $.5$ nat/token ($[1.54,3.77]\%$).
The worst intermediate change is $-1.328$ nat/token. Sequence log
probability has median change $+.154$ nat/sequence ($[+.104,+.198]$),
with $21.99\%$ of exemplars losing more than $.5$ nat/sequence.
Lengths range from 4 to 100 tokens, so the token and sequence thresholds
measure different changes.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=.95\linewidth]{figures/e121_fixed_bank_survival.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Median exemplar scores rise, while some exemplars decline.}
  Empirical cumulative distributions of first-to-final score changes for
  3,406 fixed-bank exemplars from five Qwen2.5-0.5B Re:Dr \texttt{Graph} seeds.
  Panels show mean token and sequence log probability. Colored curves
  represent individual seeds; the pooled curve weights exemplars equally.
  Dotted and dashed vertical lines mark zero and $-.5$, respectively.
  Positive changes indicate higher teacher-forced likelihood. These scores
  do not measure the probability of generating each canonical solution mode.}
  \label{fig:e121-fixed-bank-survival}
\end{figure}

Pooled summaries weight exemplars equally. Descriptive 95\% percentile
intervals use 10,000 hierarchical bootstrap replicates, resampling prompts
within each training seed and then resampling the five seeds.

Scores are observed at replay visits, rather than after every update.
The declining tail shows that some fixed exemplars lose likelihood despite
continued replay. Without a matched fixed-bank control lacking replay,
these trajectories do not identify a causal replay effect or validate the
probability floor in Theorem~\ref{thm:replay-retention}.

